        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 5 Social Engineering Attacks\nP\u00e1ginas: 131-150\n\u2022 Spear Phishing Attacks  (131)\n  \u2022 Sending an E-mail  (132)\n    \u2022 The Social Engineer Toolkit  (132)\n    \u2022 Sending an E-mail Using Python  (134)\n    \u2022 Stealing Credentials  (135)\n\u2022 Payloads and Listeners  (136)\n  \u2022 Bind Shell vs. Reverse Shell  (137)\n    \u2022 Bind Shell  (137)\n    \u2022 Reverse Shell  (138)\n    \u2022 Reverse Shell Using SET  (139)\n\u2022 Social Engineering with the USB Rubber Ducky  (141)\n  \u2022 A Practical Reverse Shell Using USB Rubber Ducky and PowerShell  (143)\n  \u2022 Generating a PowerShell Script  (144)\n    \u2022 Starting a Listener  (144)\n    \u2022 Hosting the PowerShell Script  (145)\n    \u2022 Running PowerShell  (146)\n    \u2022 Download and Execute the PS Script  (146)\n    \u2022 Reverse Shell  (147)\n    \u2022 Replicating the Attack Using the USB Rubber Ducky  (148)\n\u2022 Summary  (148)\nC H A P T E R\n105\n5\nWhen I read a book, I generally hate long introductions that don\u2019t get straight\ninto the subject. So, let\u2019s get to the point. In this chapter, you will learn about\nsocial engineering and different techniques that will help you take advantage\nof human weakness. Take note, this book is about teaching you the principles\nthat you can use in any tool installed on Kali Linux.\nIn this chapter, you will learn about the following topics in social engineering:\nI\nI Sending phishing e-mails\nI\nI Stealing credentials\nI\nI Using the Social Engineering Toolkit\nI\nI Basics of payloads and listeners\nI\nI Using the USB Rubber Ducky for social engineering attacks",
